First. For $x$ sufficiently large, $x > 0$.
Existence. Let $y \in \Real$. Then for $x$ sufficiently large, there exists a real number $z$, such that $z = x$ and $y \leq x + z$.
Disjunction. For $x$ frequently in $\atTopMACRoA$, $x < 0$ or for all real numbers $z$, $z \leq x \times z$.
Implication. For $x$ locally, if $x > 0$, then for $z$ sufficiently large, $x < z$.
Large. For $x$ sufficiently large, $1 < x$.
Close. Let $a \in \Real$. Then for $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, $x \leq a + 1$.
